<!--
  Recommended ClickHouse server settings for a binwave target.

  binwave commits the replication position only after ClickHouse acknowledged the data. By default
  ClickHouse acknowledges an insert before the new data part is flushed to disk, so a power loss or
  kernel crash of the ClickHouse host can lose acknowledged data while the position row survives:
  binwave would then continue after data that no longer exists. fsync makes the acknowledgement
  mean "on disk", at the cost of insert throughput.

  Install as /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/binwave-durability.xml. Server-level MergeTree
  settings apply to every MergeTree table, including the binwave_state checkpoint table, so no
  table definition has to change. The test environment runs with this exact file.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <merge_tree>
        <fsync_after_insert>1</fsync_after_insert>
        <fsync_part_directory>1</fsync_part_directory>
    </merge_tree>
</clickhouse>
